    "updated_at": "2025-11-26T09:05:03Z",
    "body": "I'm not convinced there's an issue here to solve. Jaws and NVDA both have keyboard commands that let you move directly to the end of a containing element, so in the scenario given above, it's possible to navigate to the `<aside>`, be informed you've arrived at some complementary content, then (should you wish) move directly to the end of the `<aside>` and continue reading the content within the `<main>` element. Jaws goes a step further and announces the end (as well as the start) of elements like `<aside>`, so you know when you've left the complementary content as you're reading (as opposed to navigating).\n\nOff the top of my head I don't know if VoiceOver or TalkBack have similar capability, but if not, then it seems like something that might be useful. Either way, this feels like a screen reader problem to solve, not a spec change.",
