    "updated_at": "2025-11-26T10:35:35Z",
    "body": "Leaving aside the argument of \"it might not work with some screen readers\" (which would be nice to actually explicitly document - and say exactly in which specific browser/SR combos there are apparent problems, rather than just handwaving it), I'd say: a document can contain multiple `<aside>`s ... relating to the same main content, or one for each `<article>`, etc. for instance, there could be multiple pull quotes marked up as asides. So the suggestion that it should live outside/adjacent to `<main>` seems illogical to me, and by extension https://dequeuniversity.com/rules/axe/4.2/landmark-complementary-is-top-level seems very \"opinionated\" and spurious to me.\n\nI'd also note that the actual definition for https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#the-aside-element says\n\n> The [aside](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#the-aside-element) element [represents](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#represents) a section of a page that consists of content that is tangentially related to the content around the [aside](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#the-aside-element) element\n\nrather than what the Deque thing says\n\n> Complementary content is ancillary content to the main theme of a document or page.\n\nIn short, I'd actually question if https://dequeuniversity.com/rules/axe/4.2/landmark-complementary-is-top-level shouldn't be changed/removed, as it sounds ... incorrect to me. *If* having nested `<aside>`s inside content causes issues for SR users, these issues should be explicitly documented, and then brought to the SR developers as bugs/feature requests for improvement at *their* end.\n\nFrom the MDN discussion, there's a claim that\n\n> When a `<aside>` is nested inside another landmark (like `<article>` or `<section>`), the nested complementary landmark may be ignored or hidden by screen readers, making the content harder or impossible to access.\n\nI've posted it over there as well https://github.com/mdn/content/issues/40664#issuecomment-3580466045 but cross-posting it here too:\n\nIs this documented properly anywhere? As in explicitly which browser/SR combinations exhibit problems here, and how do the problems manifest themselves? How is the `<aside>` \"ignored or hidden\", exactly? Is it not exposed as a landmark (and if so, note the difference between named and unnamed landmarks, and that they behave differently)? Is the whole `<aside>` somehow \"skipped\" when reading through content? What is the problem, in detail? Otherwise, this is all very handwavy at the moment...",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
