    "created_at": "2025-11-26T19:35:44Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-11-26T19:46:58Z",
    "body": "At the very least, the failing example in that axe-core documentation is incorrect and I'll file a bug to get it fixed (edit: [axe-core#4949](https://github.com/dequelabs/axe-core/issues/4949)); axe-core's `landmark-complementary-is-top-level` rule [was actually updated back in 2021](https://github.com/dequelabs/axe-core/issues/2651) to *not* fail the specific case of `<main><aside>whatever</aside></main>`. (@patrickhlauke 's link is for a pretty old version of axe-core, but [the current version of those docs](https://dequeuniversity.com/rules/axe/4.11/landmark-complementary-is-top-level) still has the same problem)\n\nI think the last long discussion about that rule's behavior was [this a11y-web slack thread](https://web-a11y.slack.com/archives/C042TSFGN/p1608139592239900) (which resulted in the above change to not fail aside-in-main). Since then, [html-aam updated the `<aside>` implicit role rules](https://github.com/w3c/html-aam/pull/350) to mitigate some of the concerns, and [aria updated the `complementary` role definition](https://github.com/w3c/aria/pull/1698) to clarify that it's explicitly intended to allow for cases where the content is \"complementary to the main content that it is a sibling to, *or a direct descendant of*\" (emphasis mine). I think this is much more clear than the \"complementary to the main content at a similar level\" wording in ARIA 1.2 that @stevefaulkner quoted; I don't think it's clear at all in the ARIA 1.2 wording that \"similar level\" would allow \"direct descendant of\" (thanks @scottaohara for clarifying the wording).\n\n@WilcoFiers, what are your thoughts here? I think the ARIA update that clarifies the intended interpretation of \"similar level\" inclines me to deprecate `landmark-complementary-is-top-level`, assuming AT testing doesn't turn up any actual current AT incompatibilities.",
